// saturation.kql
// Replica saturation pattern: QPS plateau + latency climb.
//
// Azure Search does not always return 429s under load — instead it queues
// excess requests and degrades via latency. This query surfaces that pattern:
//
//   QPS stays flat  →  the replica has hit its throughput ceiling
//   p95 climbs      →  requests are sitting in the service queue
//
// When both signals appear together, the replica is saturated. Adding replicas
// distributes the queue and brings latency back down.
//
// Use 1-minute bins during a load test to see the ramp in near-real time.
// Widen to 5m bins for production trend analysis.
//
// Columns: TimeGenerated, qps, p50_ms, p95_ms, p99_ms, total, errors_503
//
// Prerequisites:
//   - Diagnostic setting enabled on the Azure AI Search resource
//   - Category: OperationLogs → sent to this Log Analytics workspace

AzureDiagnostics
| where ResourceProvider == "MICROSOFT.SEARCH"
| where OperationName startswith "Query."
| summarize
    total      = count(),
    succeeded  = countif(resultSignature_d == 200),
    errors_503 = countif(resultSignature_d == 503),
    p50_ms     = percentile(DurationMs, 50),
    p95_ms     = percentile(DurationMs, 95),
    p99_ms     = percentile(DurationMs, 99)
    by bin(TimeGenerated, 1m)
| extend qps = round(todouble(succeeded) / 60.0, 2)
| project TimeGenerated, qps, p50_ms, p95_ms, p99_ms, total, errors_503
| order by TimeGenerated asc
// ── How to read this output ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Healthy:     qps grows with concurrency,  p95 stays low
// Saturated:   qps flat or falling,         p95 climbing steeply
// Overloaded:  errors_503 > 0               service queue exhausted
